@@ -0,0 +1,33 @@
+# 9. Due times from response-time hours
+
+Date: 2025-10-06
+Status: Accepted
+
+## Context
+
+The help desk is promising a first response by priority: 4 hours for urgent tickets, 24 for high, 72 for normal and 168
+for low. Those hours will change, so they cannot live only in code. Tickets already in the production data file need a
+due time too. A later change of the hours, or of a ticket's priority, must not move a due time that was already set. If
+the new release misbehaves, operators must be able to go back to 1.4.0 and still start on the data file this release
+wrote.
+
+## Decision
+
+We will store each ticket's due time as `due`, an ISO 8601 UTC instant, set once: created plus the configured hours for
+its priority at that moment.
+
+- The hours come from `DUE_HOURS_URGENT`, `DUE_HOURS_HIGH`, `DUE_HOURS_NORMAL` and `DUE_HOURS_LOW` (defaults 4, 24, 72,
+  168). Changing them does not rewrite tickets that already have `due`.
+- A ticket that has no `due` when it is loaded (every ticket a 1.4.0 file holds) is given one from its `created` time
+  and priority, and that value is saved. From then on it is kept.
+- The data-file version stays 1. `due` is an optional ticket field, so 1.4.0 still loads a file this release wrote: it
+  keeps the fields it knows and ignores `due`.
+- Until `DUE_TIMES` is `on`, the HTTP API is the 1.4.0 API: no `due` on tickets, and `overdue` is an unknown query
+  parameter. With `DUE_TIMES=on`, every ticket includes `due`, and `GET /tickets?overdue=true` lists the open and
+  in-progress tickets whose due time has been reached.
+
+## Consequences
+
+- Operators can fill `due` in the data file by running this release with `DUE_TIMES` unset, then switch the API on.
+- A rollback to 1.4.0 does not need a data-file rewrite.
+- `src/tickets/due.js` is the one module that knows the hours and how to tell that a ticket is overdue.
